Testing: SDD

Purpose:
  Define the test levels (T0-T7), test pyramid, test ownership, and
  the BDD → test chain that connects requirements to automated tests.
  This file is the testing lifecycle engine — it defines WHAT is tested
  at WHAT level and WHO owns it, not HOW to write individual tests
  (that belongs to skills/testing/).

  Testing is part of STLC (Software Testing Lifecycle), which is embedded
  inside SDLC (Software Development Lifecycle). The test pyramid ensures
  fast feedback at the lowest possible level.


Owns:
  ./


Scope:
  Test levels T0-T7
  Test pyramid
  Test ownership per level
  BDD to test chain
  Test coverage requirements
  Test failure routing


DoesNotOwn:
  Project-specific test suites (bax project/<domain>/tests/)
  Engineering skills (bax skills/testing/)
  Task instances (bax tasks/)
  QA stage behavior (bax workflow/stages.sdd -> QA)


TestLevels:

  T0: Unit Test
    Scope:     Single function, method, or component in isolation
    Owner:     Implementer (BE/FE/MD/DB)
    Framework: Project-specific (pytest, Jest, JUnit, etc.)
    Speed:     fast (< 1s per test)
    When:      during implementation, before commit
    Gate:      all unit tests pass before stage gate

  T1: Integration Test
    Scope:     Multiple components working together
    Owner:     Implementer (BE/FE/MD/DB)
    Framework: Project-specific
    Speed:     medium (1-10s per test)
    When:      after unit tests pass, before QA stage
    Gate:      all integration tests pass before QA stage gate

  T2: Contract Test
    Scope:     API contract, interface compatibility
    Owner:     API + consuming stage (BE/FE/MD)
    Framework: Pact, OpenAPI validator, custom
    Speed:     fast-medium
    When:      after API stage, before FE/MD
    Gate:      all contract tests pass before FE/MD stage gate

  T3: Component Test
    Scope:     A complete feature or module in isolation
    Owner:     Implementer (BE/FE/MD)
    Framework: Project-specific, often same as unit
    Speed:     medium
    When:      after integration tests, before QA
    Gate:      component test pass before QA stage gate

  T4: E2E Test
    Scope:     Complete user journey across all layers
    Owner:     QA
    Framework: Playwright, Cypress, Selenium
    Speed:     slow (10s+ per test)
    When:      after QA stage, before SC
    Gate:      critical E2E paths pass before SC stage gate

  T5: Performance Test
    Scope:     Load, stress, spike, soak
    Owner:     QA + DO
    Framework: k6, JMeter, Gatling
    Speed:     varies by duration
    When:      after SC stage, before DO
    Gate:      performance meets NFR targets before DO stage gate

  T6: Security Test
    Scope:     SAST, DAST, dependency scan, penetration test
    Owner:     SC
    Framework: OWASP ZAP, Snyk, Trivy, custom
    Speed:     varies
    When:      after QA stage, before DO
    Gate:      no CRITICAL/HIGH findings before DO stage gate

  T7: Acceptance Test
    Scope:     Business acceptance criteria, user validation
    Owner:     QA + Business
    Framework: BDD runner, manual
    Speed:     varies
    When:      after DO, before VR
    Gate:      all acceptance criteria met before VR stage gate


TestPyramid:

  The test pyramid defines the expected distribution of test types:

        /\        T7: Acceptance (few)
       /  \       T6: Security (as needed)
      /    \      T5: Performance (as needed)
     /      \     T4: E2E (some)
    /________\    T3: Component (more)
                 T2: Contract (more)
                 T1: Integration (many)
                 T0: Unit (most)

  AI MUST NOT invert the pyramid — a project with more E2E tests than
  unit tests has a testing anti-pattern (bax AntiPatterns in skills/
  skills.sdd).

  Minimum ratios (guidelines, not hard rules):
    Unit:      60-70% of all tests
    Integration: 15-20%
    Component: 10-15%
    E2E:       5-10%
    Performance/Security/Acceptance: as needed by risk


TestOwnership:

  Each test level has a single owner responsible for its quality:

    T0  Implementer (BE/FE/MD/DB) — owns unit tests for their code
    T1  Implementer (BE/FE/MD/DB) — owns integration tests for their components
    T2  API owner + consumer — owns contract tests for their interface
    T3  Implementer (BE/FE/MD) — owns component tests for their feature
    T4  QA — owns E2E test suite
    T5  QA + DO — owns performance tests
    T6  SC — owns security tests
    T7  QA + Business — owns acceptance tests

  AI MUST NOT write tests for a level owned by another role without
  their involvement — tests are code, and code ownership rules apply.


BDDToTestChain:

  BDD scenarios (Given/When/Then) are the bridge between requirements
  and automated tests:

    BDD Scenario
      |
      v
    T0: Unit Test (per step, per component)
      |
      v
    T1: Integration Test (per scenario, across components)
      |
      v
    T2: Contract Test (per API interaction)
      |
      v
    T3: Component Test (per feature)
      |
      v
    T4: E2E Test (per user journey)
      |
      v
    T5/T6: Performance/Security (per risk)
      |
      v
    T7: Acceptance Test (per business criterion)

  AI MUST NOT skip levels in this chain without human approval — each
  level catches different failure modes.


BDDToTaskChain:

  BDD scenarios drive task creation and validation:

    BDD Scenario
      |
      v
    Task (implementation)
      |
      v
    T0-T3: Automated tests (implementer)
      |
      v
    QA stage: T4-T6 (QA/SC/DO)
      |
      v
    T7: Acceptance (QA + Business)
      |
      v
    VR: Final verification


TestCoverage:

  AI MUST track test coverage per level:

    Coverage:
      T0:  >= 80% line coverage
      T1:  all critical integration paths
      T2:  all public API contracts
      T3:  all features
      T4:  all critical user journeys
      T5:  all NFR targets
      T6:  all security-critical paths
      T7:  all acceptance criteria

  Coverage thresholds are project-specific — this file defines the
  levels, project/flows.sdd defines the thresholds.


TestFailure:

  Test failure MUST route to the responsible stage:

    T0 failure   -> Implementer (code defect)
    T1 failure   -> Implementer (integration defect)
    T2 failure   -> API owner (contract defect)
    T3 failure   -> Implementer (feature defect)
    T4 failure   -> BE/FE/MD/DB (implementation defect) or QA (test gap)
    T5 failure   -> BE/DO (performance defect)
    T6 failure   -> SC/Implementer (security defect)
    T7 failure   -> HUMAN (acceptance criteria not met)


Rules:

  [TEST1] Every feature MUST have at least one BDD scenario before
          implementation begins.

  [TEST2] Every BDD scenario MUST map to at least one automated test.

  [TEST3] Unit tests (T0) MUST be written before or concurrently with
          implementation — test-after is forbidden for new code.

  [TEST4] Contract tests (T2) MUST be written before the consumer
          implements against the contract.

  [TEST5] E2E tests (T4) MUST cover all critical user journeys.

  [TEST6] Security tests (T6) MUST be included for any feature with
          security impact (bax ImpactMatrix in tasks/tasks.sdd).

  [TEST7] Performance tests (T5) MUST be included when NFR targets are
          defined.

  [TEST8] Acceptance tests (T7) MUST be written in business language,
          not technical language.

  [TEST9] Test ownership MUST be explicit — AI MUST NOT write tests for
          a level owned by another role without their involvement.

  [TEST10] Test failure MUST route to the responsible owner, not to the
          next test level.

  [TEST11] AI MUST NOT skip test levels without human approval.

  [TEST12] The test pyramid MUST NOT be inverted — more E2E tests than
          unit tests is an anti-pattern.

  [TEST13] Test code MUST follow the same quality standards as
          production code (lint, review, etc.).


State: +
  